<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanjaya of Mataram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Rakai Mataram Sang Ratu Sanjaya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanjaya was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> in the early 8th century, regarded as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom</a> of Mataram. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canggal-inscription">Canggal inscription</a> of 732 records him erecting a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> lingam, and later kings, such as those listed in the Mantyasih inscription of 907, traced their line to him. Historians often speak of a Sanjaya dynasty alongside the Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendras</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanjaya-of-mataram.jpg" alt="Sanjaya of Mataram" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canggal_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Written in Sanskrit in the Pallava script, it tells of a lingga raised on a hill in the Kunjarakunja area of Yawadwipa, a noble island rich in rice and gold, on the orders of <b>Rakai Mataram Sang Ratu Sanjaya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people and court traditions are thought to have passed into the Medang Kingdom, founded by <b>Sanjaya</b> in 732 in the interior of Central Java.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalingga-kingdom">Kalingga Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Samaratungga's daughter Pramodhawardhani married Rakai Pikatan of the <b>Sanjaya</b> line, and the Sailendras lost their hold on Central Java in the mid-9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/sailendra-kingdom">Sailendra Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
